'''Shane Geoffrey Jones''' (born 3 September 1959) is a New Zealand politician. He is deputy leader of [[New Zealand First]] and a Member of Parliament (MP) in the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]].

Jones's political career began [[2005 New Zealand general election|in 2005]] as a [[list MP]] for the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]. He became a [[cabinet minister]] in his first term, serving as [[Minister for Building and Construction (New Zealand)|Minister for Building and Construction]] in the [[Fifth Labour Government of New Zealand]]. Following Labour's defeat in the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 election]], he was a senior opposition MP and unsuccessfully contested the leadership of the Labour Party in a [[2013 New Zealand Labour Party leadership election|2013 leadership election]].<ref name="Jones challenges">{{cite news |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz-labour-party/news/article.cfm?o_id=264&objectid=11114293 |title=Jones' hat in ring to lead Labour |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |last=Trevett |first=Claire |date=22 August 2013 |access-date=16 September 2013}}</ref><ref name="Cunliffe wins">{{cite news |url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/9166765/Cunliffe-wins-Labour-leadership|title=Cunliffe wins Labour leadership |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=15 September 2013 |access-date=15 September 2013}}</ref> He left parliament the following year for a brief diplomatic career,<ref name="trevett">{{Cite news |last=Trevett |first=Claire |date=22 April 2014 |title=Labour MP Shane Jones to step down |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11242350}}</ref> before returning as a New Zealand First MP at the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]].

Jones was Minister for Regional Economic Development and [[Minister of Forestry (New Zealand)|Minister of Forestry]] in the [[Sixth Labour Government of New Zealand|Labour–New Zealand First coalition government]] from 2017 to 2020. He was elected for a fifth non-consecutive term in Parliament at the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]], and is [[Minister for Oceans and Fisheries]], Minister for Regional Development, and Minister for Resources in the [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand|National–ACT–New Zealand First coalition government]].

==Early life and career==
Jones was born in Awanui, near Kaitaia, the eldest of six children to parents Peter, a farmer, and Ruth, a teacher.<ref name=beehive2008/><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Jones, Shane: Address in Reply – New Zealand Parliament |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/document/48HansS_20051115_00000647/jones-shane-address-in-reply |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=www.parliament.nz |language=en}}</ref> He is [[Māori people|Māori]], of [[Te Aupōuri]] and [[Ngāi Takoto]] descent, as well as having [[English people|English]], [[Welsh New Zealanders|Welsh]] and [[Croatian New Zealanders|Croatian]] ancestry.<ref name=beehive2008>{{cite web |url= http://www.beehive.govt.nz/minister/shane+jones?bio=1 |title=Hon Shane Jones|publisher=New Zealand Government |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20081015150848/http://beehive.govt.nz/minister/shane+jones?bio=1|archive-date=15 October 2008|access-date=16 March 2014}}</ref><ref name=Ralston>{{cite journal |url=http://www.listener.co.nz/commentary/the-man-from-mangonui |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141129034621/http://www.listener.co.nz/commentary/the-man-from-mangonui |url-status=dead |archive-date=29 November 2014 |title=The Man from Mangonui |last=Ralston |first=Bill |journal=[[New Zealand Listener]] |volume=208 |issue=3501|date=16 June 2007}}</ref> 
Jones's secondary education was at [[Tīpene / St Stephen's School|St Stephen's School]], a boarding school for Māori boys in [[Bombay, New Zealand|Bombay]], south of [[Auckland]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=Jones, Shane: Valedictory Statements – New Zealand Parliament |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/document/50HansS_20140521_00001205/jones-shane-valedictory-statements |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=www.parliament.nz |language=en}}</ref> His time there overlapped with future [[Māori Party]] MPs [[Hone Harawira]] and [[Te Ururoa Flavell]].<ref name=":2" /> He next studied at [[Victoria University of Wellington]] where he earned a Bachelor of Arts.<ref name=":2" /> In 1990, he was awarded a [[Harkness Fellowship]] to study at [[Harvard Kennedy School]] at [[Harvard University]] where he completed a Master of Public Administration.<ref name=":2" /><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/44900/Jones-nets-Cabinet-post|title=Jones nets Cabinet post|date=31 January 2009|publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] }}</ref> Jones returned to Victoria University in the 1990s as a lecturer in Māori studies.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Metge |first1=Joan |last2=Jones |first2=Shane |date=1 January 1995 |title=He Taonga Tuku Ihō no Ngā Tūpuna: Māori Proverbial Sayings — a Literary Treasure |url=https://ojs.victoria.ac.nz/jnzs/article/view/471 |journal=The Journal of New Zealand Studies |language=en |volume=5 |issue=2 |doi=10.26686/jnzs.v5i2.471 |issn=2324-3740|doi-access=free }}</ref> He is fluent in [[Māori language|te reo Māori]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Harman |first=Richard |date=2023-10-20 |title=NZ First goes backwards {{!}} Politik |url=https://www.politik.co.nz/nz-first-goes-backwards/ |access-date=2023-12-06 |language=en-NZ}}</ref>

Jones was a public servant in the 1980s. He worked in the Māori secretariat in the [[Ministry for the Environment (New Zealand)|Ministry for the Environment]] and later in the [[Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet (New Zealand)|Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet]], providing advice to the [[Fourth Labour Government of New Zealand|Fourth Labour Government]] on [[Treaty of Waitangi claims and settlements|settling Treaty of Waitangi breaches]]. When the [[Fourth National Government of New Zealand|Fourth National Government]] began the settlements process in the 1990s, he was appointed a member of the [[Treaty of Waitangi Fisheries Commission]]. He became chair of the commission in August 2000 and completed the allocation of fisheries resources among iwi in 2004.<ref name=":1">{{Cite news |last=Milne |first=Jonathan |title=Jones: 'Right man in the wrong party' |url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/kahu/jones-the-right-man-in-the-wrong-party/MCINTLV2DQ6ZFCT2SHNN5GDSNI/ |date=27 April 2014 |access-date=17 November 2022 |newspaper=Herald on Sunday |via=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ}}</ref> He also chaired the Māori-owned fishing corporation [[Sealord Group|Sealord]] during which period the company was merged with [[Nippon Suisan Kaisha]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sealord Purchased For NZ$207,750,000 |url=https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/BU0012/S00022/sealord-purchased-for-nz207750000.htm |date=5 December 2000 |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=www.scoop.co.nz}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Louisson |first=Simon |title=Sealord's job catch undersized so far |date=19 January 2003 |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/sealords-job-catch-undersized-so-far/Z4NYJSHMIYX2C47BADJ7QAG6IU/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ}}</ref> A 2004 "[[New Zealand Listener Power List|power list]]" by the ''[[New Zealand Listener]]'' ranked him the ninth most powerful New Zealander.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Grant |first=Alexis  |date=4 December 2004 |title=Scribe's got the power |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/scribes-got-the-power/HNS5R4JK7SJZFPD7IHOSZ5BLLI/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ}}</ref>

