{{End}}Jones has been elected to Parliament for two political parties—first with the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] for nine years from 2005 to 2014, then with [[New Zealand First]] from 2017 to 2020—and was a Cabinet minister under both affiliations. Although he had often been speculated by the media and among his colleagues as a future leader of the Labour Party,<ref name="Ralston" /> and indeed [[2013 New Zealand Labour Party leadership election|contested the Labour leadership in 2013]], Jones's move away from Labour was not a surprise. Before his election as a Labour MP, Sir [[Graham Latimer]] had tried, unsuccessfully, to recruit him for the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]].<ref name=":1" /> Instead, Jones joined Labour in part because he had been impressed by [[David Lange]] and the [[Fourth Labour Government of New Zealand|Fourth Labour Government]].<ref name=":0" /> Over time he felt less comfortable in the "modern Labour Party," and openly stated in 2014 that he was not "naturally left-leaning."<ref name=":1" /> He had been speculated as a New Zealand First candidate since at least 2015 before joining the party in 2017, in part due to his close relationship with New Zealand First leader [[Winston Peters]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Husband |first=Dale |date=5 December 2015 |title=Shane Jones: No silver spoon. No shyness either. |url=https://e-tangata.co.nz/korero/shane-jones-no-silver-spoon-no-shyness-either/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=E-Tangata |language=en-NZ}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Moir |first=Jo |date=3 September 2016 |title=The NZ First succession plan: Shane Jones vs Ron Mark |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/83749710/the-nz-first-succession-plan-shane-jones-vs-ron-mark |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=30 June 2017 |title=Shane Jones to stand for NZ First in Whangarei |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/334145/shane-jones-to-stand-for-nz-first-in-whangarei |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=[[RNZ]] |language=en-nz}}</ref>[[File:Shane Jones New Zealand MP.jpg|thumb|left|Jones in 2007|216x216px]]

===Fifth Labour Government, 2005–2008===
After the fisheries settlement was passed by Parliament in 2004, Jones announced he was interested in standing for the Labour Party at the [[2005 New Zealand general election|upcoming election]]. He was approved as the candidate for the [[Northland (New Zealand electorate)|Northland]] electorate and was ranked 27 on the party list. This was the highest position given by Labour to someone who was not already a member of Parliament. While Jones did not win Northland, he entered Parliament as a list MP and was immediately made the chair of the [[Finance and Expenditure Committee|Finance and Expenditure Select Committee]].

On 31 October 2007, by then still in his first term, Jones was promoted into Cabinet. He became [[Minister for Building and Construction (New Zealand)|Minister for Building and Construction]] and held additional responsibilities as associate minister in charge of Treaty of Waitangi Negotiations, Immigration and Trade. He scrapped a government proposal requiring new buildings to have low flow showers heads, prior to the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 general election]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Gibson |first=Eloise |date=15 October 2008 |title=Low flow shower plan down the gurgler |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10537528 |access-date=12 June 2010}}</ref>

=== Opposition, 2008–2014 ===
Labour was defeated at the election and Jones contested the [[Northland (New Zealand electorate)|Northland electorate]] unsuccessfully, but was returned to parliament as a list member due to his high [[Party lists in the 2008 New Zealand general election|list placing of 16]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Party Lists of Successful Registered Parties |url=https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2008/e9/html/e9_part3_1.html |access-date=1 December 2019 |website=www.electionresults.govt.nz}}</ref> Labour lost again in the 2011 election; Jones was defeated in [[Tāmaki Makaurau (New Zealand electorate)|Tāmaki Makaurau]] but remained a list MP. During his six years as an opposition MP, Jones held various portfolios in the [[Shadow Cabinet of Phil Goff|Goff]], [[Shadow Cabinet of David Shearer|Shearer]] and [[Shadow Cabinet of David Cunliffe|Cunliffe]] shadow cabinets including building and construction, infrastructure, economic development, transport, fisheries, forestry and Māori affairs.

He was twice removed from his portfolios under controversy. On 10 June 2010, after the release of ministerial credit card records, Jones admitted to having used a [[The Crown|Crown]] credit card for personal expenditure, but assured the public that he had reimbursed the Crown in full for the expenditure. Later that day Jones admitted that he had used the card to hire pornographic films at hotels while on ministerial business.<ref>{{cite news |date=10 June 2010 |title=Shane Jones talks about porn scandal |work=[[Newshub|3 News]] |url=http://www.3news.co.nz/Shane-Jones-talks-about-porn-scandal-/tabid/370/articleID/160197/Default.aspx |access-date=10 June 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120908070649/http://www.3news.co.nz/Shane-Jones-talks-about-porn-scandal-/tabid/370/articleID/160197/Default.aspx |archive-date=8 September 2012}}</ref> The credit card record showed that he chartered an executive jet for $1200, which he claimed was due to bad weather which forced a change in his schedule.<ref>{{cite news |date=10 June 2010 |title=Shane Jones, Minister of Pornography |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/3796622/Shane-Jones-Minister-of-Pornography |access-date=10 June 2010}}</ref> Four days later, opposition leader [[Phil Goff]] demoted Jones along with two other Labour MPs for misuse of ministerial credit cards.<ref>{{cite news |date=15 June 2010 |title=Rising stars to replace shamed trio |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=10651965 |access-date=15 June 2010}}</ref>

As Associate Minister of Immigration in 2008, Jones approved the [[citizenship]] application of Labour Party donor, Chinese businessman and later convicted money launderer William Yan, also known as Bill Liu. Four years later, Yan was charged with making false declarations on immigration documents. On 23 May 2012, Jones stood down from the front bench and his [[Shadow Cabinet|shadow portfolios]] while an investigation took place. Labour Party leader [[David Shearer]] asked the [[Controller and Auditor-General of New Zealand|Auditor-General]] to investigate Jones's handling of the citizenship application. Jones had acted against officials' advice that he should decline the application because of questions about Yan's multiple identities and a warrant for his arrest in China. Jones defended his decision, saying it was based on humanitarian grounds because a high-level Government official had told him that Yan faced execution if he returned to China. Shearer said Jones supported the decision to refer the matter to the Auditor-General because Jones must be given a chance to clear his name.<ref>{{cite news |date=23 May 2012 |title=Businessman not guilty of fraud |language=en-NZ |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10808099 |access-date=31 March 2020}}</ref><ref name="Trevett">{{Cite web |title=Shearer stands Shane Jones down |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/shearer-stands-shane-jones-down/XDAS6E3RPZX2T46TD5Q55WTE6M/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=May 2012 |language=en-NZ}}</ref> On 24 May 2012, Yan was found not guilty on all the immigration charges.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Field |first=Michael |date=23 May 2012 |title=Not guilty decision in Yong Ming Yan Case |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/dominion-post/news/politics/6977210/Not-guilty-decision-in-Yong-Ming-Yan-Case |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en}}</ref> The Auditor-General investigation commenced on 30 May 2012.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Auditor General to investigate Jones |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/auditor-general-to-investigate-jones/7GBJNPEER6Z3WLG3BDWJU36RWY/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=21 May 2012 |language=en-NZ}}</ref> When it reported back the following year, it found no evidence that there was any improper motive, collusion, or political interference in the decision to authorise citizenship.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Inquiry into decision by Hon Shane Jones to grant citizenship to Mr Yang Liu |url=https://oag.parliament.nz/2013/citizenship/docs/inquiry-citizenship.pdf/view |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=Office of the Auditor-General New Zealand |language=en}}</ref>

In 2014, while economic development spokesperson, Jones alleged that [[Progressive Enterprises]], owner of [[Countdown (supermarket)|Countdown supermarkets]], was involved in racketeering and extortion.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Jones accuses Countdown of corruption |url=https://www.interest.co.nz/news/68457/labour-mp-shane-jones-accuses-countdown-parliament-corruption-racketeering-and-acting |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=interest.co.nz |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Rutherford |first=Hamish |date=13 February 2014 |title=Countdown allegations 'very serious' – Minister |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/ipad-editors-picks/9714885/Countdown-allegations-very-serious-Minister |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Shane Jones: I'm vindicated – New Zealand News |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/shane-jones-im-vindicated/VJBS7ZU6L7T7UIB3CXQRPZGIBM/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=20 February 2014 |language=en-NZ}}</ref> A [[Commerce Commission]] investigation found no evidence to support the claims.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Countdown off the hook |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/no-evidence-of-intimidating-suppliers-countdown-off-the-hook/VPYVTWDGON6QKLFMIEKMZHJ4NI/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=18 February 2014 |language=en-NZ}}</ref>

=== Leadership contest and resignation ===
[[David Shearer]] resigned the Labour leadership in August 2013. Jones was the second MP to declare his candidacy, following Shearer's former deputy leader Grant Robertson. The pair would be joined by the party's economic development spokesperson [[David Cunliffe]], who secured sufficient support from party delegates to win the leadership. Jones later said his candidacy was to honour Labour stalwart [[Parekura Horomia]] who had died earlier that year. Jones won the votes of seven out of 34 caucus colleagues and a minority of members' and affiliated unions' votes. Crucially, he did not have the support of senior Māori Labour MPs [[Nanaia Mahuta]] and [[Louisa Wall]].<ref name=":1" /> Ten years later, ''[[The Spinoff]]'' founder [[Duncan Greive|Duncan Grieve]] would write Jones's candidacy was less an "expectation of success than [a] signal he felt his banishment had ended."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Greive |first=Duncan |date=2023-10-07 |title=The rise and fall and fall and rise and fall and rise and fall and rise of Shane Jones |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/07-10-2023/the-rise-and-fall-and-fall-and-rise-and-fall-and-rise-and-fall-and-rise-of-shane-jones |access-date=2023-11-12 |website=[[The Spinoff]] |language=en}}</ref>

On 22 April 2014, Jones announced his intention to step down as a Labour Party MP, leaving at the end of May. He was appointed to the newly created role of Pacific Economic Ambassador by Foreign Affairs Minister [[Murray McCully]].<ref name="trevett" /> [[Kelvin Davis (politician)|Kelvin Davis]] succeeded him as Labour list MP.

==New Zealand First, 2017&ndash;present==
[[File:Shane Jones 2018 1.jpg|thumb|Shane Jones (Economic Development Minister) at an event at [[Victoria University of Wellington]] in 2018]]

===2017&ndash;2020 term===
On 30 June 2017, after months of speculation, Jones was confirmed as the [[New Zealand First]] candidate for [[Whangarei (New Zealand electorate)|Whangarei]] for the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/94261885/shane-jones-confirmed-as-nz-first-candidate-for-whangarei|title=Shane Jones confirmed as NZ First candidate for Whangarei|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=30 June 2017 |access-date=30 June 2017}}</ref> Jones was also placed eighth on the party list for New Zealand First, above some of the members of the New Zealand First caucus of the [[51st New Zealand Parliament|Parliament at the time]],  increasing his chances of re-entering Parliament.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11913244|title=Shane Jones in eighth place as NZ First reveals its list|website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=29 August 2017 |access-date=12 September 2017}}</ref>
New Zealand online magazine ''[[The Spinoff]]'' hosted a live debate on Facebook with seven of the 2017 election's candidates that the magazine found "most exciting", including Jones, representing New Zealand First.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/06-09-2017/the-spinoff-great-debate-7pm-tonight-on-facebook-live/|title=The Spinoff Great Debate – 7pm Tonight on Facebook Live|website=[[The Spinoff]] |date=6 September 2017 |access-date=12 September 2017}}</ref> Jones placed third in Whangarei, behind [[New Zealand National Party|National]] candidate [[Shane Reti]] and Labour candidate Tony Savage,<ref>{{cite web |title=Whangarei – Official Result |url=https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/electorate-details-63.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=5 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200117212958/http://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/electorate-details-63.html |archive-date=17 January 2020 |url-status=live}}</ref> but was elected as one of New Zealand First's nine list MPs.<ref>{{cite web |title=2017 General Election – Official Result Successful Candidates |url=https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/successful-candidates.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=5 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200117212450/http://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/successful-candidates.html |archive-date=17 January 2020 |url-status=live}}</ref>

New Zealand First held the balance of power. Jones was part of the negotiating team that ultimately saw Winston Peters select a coalition with Labour over National. Jones was appointed [[Minister for Infrastructure (New Zealand)|Minister for Infrastructure]], Minister of Forestry and Minister for Regional Economic Development, [[Associate Minister of Finance (New Zealand)|Associate Minister of Finance]] and Associate Minister of Transport.<ref>{{cite web|title=Ministerial List|url=https://www.dpmc.govt.nz/our-business-units/cabinet-office/ministers-and-their-portfolios/ministerial-list|publisher=[[Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet (New Zealand)|Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet]]|access-date=26 October 2017}}</ref> As Minister for Regional Economic Development Jones was responsible for the $3 billion Provincial Growth Fund and announced a number of grants for the development of various regions, e.g. for Southland, the West Coast, the Wairarapa and [[Kaipara District]].<ref>{{cite web |date=November 2018 |title=Labour-led government 2017–2020 regional economic development |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/portfolio/labour-led-government-2017-2020/regional-economic-development |publisher=The Beehive}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=13 March 2018 |title=Shane Jones reveals the panel who will help steer the $3b Provincial Growth Fund |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/102221349/shane-jones-reveals-the-panel-who-will-help-steer-the-3b-provincial-growth-fund |publisher=Stuff (Fairfax)}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=1 August 2018 |title=Southlanders in running for slice of $3-billion Provincial Growth Fund |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/105882326/southlanders-in-running-for-slice-of-3-billion-provincial-growth-fund |publisher=Stuff (Fairfax)}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Walls |first1=Jason |title=Prime Minister Jacinda Ardern announces $100m funding package for Māori development and infrastructure |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12200399 |access-date=5 February 2019 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=3 February 2019 |archive-date=5 February 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190205075038/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12200399 |url-status=live }}</ref> The first grants in February 2018 included $6 million for the Whanganui rail line, $5 million for the Napier-Wairoa rail line and $2.3 million for the Gisborne port.<ref>{{cite web |date=23 February 2018 |title=Shane Jones doles out millions to Northland, Hawkes Bay and (to) rail regeneration |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/101706620/shane-jones-doles-out-millions-to-northland-hawkes-bay-and-rail-regeneration?rm=m |publisher=Stuff (Fairfax)}}</ref> Further grants were announced for [[Hillside Engineering]] in [[South Dunedin]] ($20{{Nbsp}}million) as a major heavy engineering and [[KiwiRail]] servicing hub.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McNeilly |first1=Hamish |date=30 October 2019 |title=Dunedin projects secure multimillion-dollar Provincial Growth Fund investment |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/business/117015551/dunedins-hillside-railway-workshop-back-on-track-with-provincial-growth-fund-boost |access-date=31 October 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Loughrey |first1=David |date=30 October 2019 |title=$20m to revitalise 'vital' Hillside Workshop |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/20m-revitalise-vital-hillside-workshop |access-date=31 October 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=30 October 2019 |title=PGF payout: Dunedin gets $40m for Hillside workshop, waterfront |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/402093/pgf-payout-dunedin-gets-40m-for-hillside-workshop-waterfront |access-date=31 October 2019}}</ref> The fund was criticised by National for being a "slush fund" targeted toward marginal electorates and for the links between some fund applications and New Zealand First.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 October 2020 |title=The Provincial Growth Fund's last ride |url=https://www.newsroom.co.nz/page/the-provincial-growth-funds-last-ride |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=Newsroom |language=en-AU}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=21 January 2020 |title=Shane Jones must explain his shady PGF meetings |work=[[New Zealand National Party]] |publisher=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]] |url=https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA2001/S00051/shane-jones-must-explain-his-shady-pgf-meetings.htm |access-date=21 January 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=21 January 2020 |title=National calls for investigation into Shane Jones and forestry company |work=[[1News]] |url=https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/national-calls-investigation-into-shane-jones-and-forestry-company |access-date=21 January 2020}}</ref>

Jones continued to court controversy in his new party. On 25 September 2019, Jones and Labour MP [[Kieran McAnulty]] were ejected from Parliament by the [[Speaker of the New Zealand House of Representatives|Speaker of the House]] [[Trevor Mallard]] after trading barbs with National MPs during a parliamentary debate about [[Prime Minister of New Zealand|Prime Minister]] [[Jacinda Ardern]]'s meeting with [[President of the United States|US President]] [[Donald Trump]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Small |first1=Zane |title=Labour MP Kieran McAnulty booted from House for mocking Simon Bridges |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2019/09/labour-mp-kieran-mcanulty-booted-from-house-for-mocking-simon-bridges.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190925221219/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2019/09/labour-mp-kieran-mcanulty-booted-from-house-for-mocking-simon-bridges.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=25 September 2019 |access-date=26 September 2019 |publisher=[[Newshub]] |date=25 September 2019}}</ref>

As Forestry Minister, Jones's flagship policy was to plant one billion trees. A farmers' protest in November 2019 against the Government's forestry policy caught Jones's ire; he described them as "rednecks."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Desmarais |first1=Felix |last2=Devlin |first2=Collette |date=14 November 2019 |title=Shane Jones calls protesting farmers 'rednecks' following rowdy march on Parliament |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/117426550/shane-jones-calls-protesting-farmers-rednecks-following-rowdy-march-on-parliament |access-date=15 November 2019}}</ref> [[Federated Farmers]] vice president [[Andrew Hoggard]] described Jones's comments as unhelpful and alleged that the Government was ignoring the agricultural sector's concerns.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Walls |first1=Jason |title=NZ First Minister Shane Jones calls hundreds of farmer protesters outside Parliament 'rednecks' |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12285201 |access-date=15 November 2019 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=14 November 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Quinlivan |first1=Mark |last2=Herbert |first2=Delphine |title=Shane Jones' redneck comments 'remarkable show of disrespect' – Todd Muller |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/rural/2019/11/shane-jones-redneck-comments-remarkable-show-of-disrespect-todd-muller.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191115093323/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/rural/2019/11/shane-jones-redneck-comments-remarkable-show-of-disrespect-todd-muller.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=15 November 2019 |access-date=15 November 2019 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=14 November 2019}}</ref> In 2020, Jones described climate change activists for advocating reduced meat consumption as "medieval torture chamber workers" hellbent on "preaching this gospel of absolutism" in response to the Government's recent announcement that they would be introducing [[climate change education]] in schools.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shane Jones unleashes on 'bible-bashing' climate change activists |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/01/shane-jones-unleashes-on-bible-bashing-climate-change-activists.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200121011600/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/01/shane-jones-unleashes-on-bible-bashing-climate-change-activists.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=21 January 2020 |access-date=21 January 2020 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=20 January 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Walls |first1=Jason |title=NZ First MP and Minister Shane Jones takes aim at 'eco-bible-bashing' climate-change activists |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12301929 |access-date=21 January 2020 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=20 January 2020}}</ref>

===Out of Parliament, 2020&ndash;2023===
Jones was selected as New Zealand First's [[Northland (New Zealand electorate)|Northland]] candidate for the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]]. He was defeated, coming third place with 5,119 votes behind Labour's [[Willow-Jean Prime]] (17,066) and National's [[Matt King (politician)|Matt King]] (16,903).<ref>{{cite web |title=Northland – Official Result|url=https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/electorate-details-32.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=6 November 2020 }}</ref><ref name="Stuff Election 2020">{{cite news |last1=Manch |first1=Thomas |last2=Jancic |first2=Boris |title=Election 2020: Shane Jones drowns his sorrows during harrowing night for NZ First |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300135655/election-2020-shane-jones-drowns-his-sorrows-during-harrowing-night-for-nz-first |access-date=18 October 2020 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=18 October 2020|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201018222029/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300135655/election-2020-shane-jones-drowns-his-sorrows-during-harrowing-night-for-nz-first|archive-date=18 October 2020}}</ref> New Zealand First also lost all its parliamentary seats, gaining only 2.6% percent of the party vote, below the five percent threshold needed to enter Parliament.<ref>{{cite web |title=2020 General Election and Referendums – Official Result Nationwide Party Votes Results |url=https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/ |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=6 November 2020}}</ref> The day after the election, Jones, hungover and dressed in a t-shirt and baseball cap, was one of the first New Zealand First MPs to address media, saying he was "astounded" with Labour's success in the election.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jancic |first=Thomas Manch and Boris |date=2020-10-18 |title=Election 2020: Shane Jones drowns his sorrows during harrowing night for NZ First |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300135655/election-2020-shane-jones-drowns-his-sorrows-during-harrowing-night-for-nz-first |access-date=2023-11-12 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en}}</ref> Since leaving Parliament for the second time, Jones has provided media commentary critical of the [[Sixth Labour Government of New Zealand|Sixth Labour Government]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Shane Jones: A rerun of St Jacinda and Labouring on with salami politics |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/shane-jones-labouring-on-with-salami-policies/7RBJT5MJNVFXZOCP34YGNEMCHY/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=10 November 2022 |language=en-NZ}}</ref>

In the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]], Jones stood as New Zealand First's [[Northland (New Zealand electorate)|Northland]] candidate and was ranked second on the party list. During the campaign, he was described as New Zealand First's deputy leader and finance spokesperson and advocated for greater investment in regional New Zealand.<ref>{{Cite web |title=An interview with Shane Jones |url=https://www.interest.co.nz/public-policy/124532/new-zealand-first-deputy-leader-and-finance-spokesperson-shane-jones-discusses |access-date=2023-11-12 |website=interest.co.nz |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-10-15 |title=Shane Jones on NZ First returning to Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/morningreport/audio/2018911196/shane-jones-on-nz-first-returning-to-parliament |access-date=2023-11-12 |website=[[RNZ]] |language=en-nz}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gifford |first=Adam |date=2023-10-24 |title=NZ First eyes regional rebuild |url=https://waateanews.com/2023/10/25/nz-first-eyes-regional-rebuild/ |access-date=2023-11-12 |website=Waatea News: Māori Radio Station |language=en-NZ}}</ref> (He was not officially elected as deputy leader of New Zealand First until September 2025.<ref name="r152">{{cite web |date=26 March 2024 |title=New Zealand First Caucus Announces Shane Jones As Deputy Leader |url=https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA2509/S00102/new-zealand-first-caucus-announces-shane-jones-as-deputy-leader.htm |access-date=17 September 2025 |website=Scoop News}}</ref>) In mid-August 2023, he released a [[TikTok]] video of himself singing to the tune of the American rock band [[Journey (band)|Journey]]'s song "[[Don't Stop Believin']]". Jones also used the song to highlight his role in promoting the Government's Provincial Growth Fund.<ref>{{cite news |title=Election 2023: Shane Jones appeals to Northland voters through TikTok song |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/election-2023-shane-jones-appeals-to-northland-voters-through-tiktok-song/MPI2AFDZKVDFLIEW6HLVEMFP6M/ |access-date=5 September 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=19 August 2023}}</ref> He polled in third place in the electorate.<ref>{{cite web |title=Northland – Official Result |url=https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/electorate-details-32.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=25 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231123104119/https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/electorate-details-32.html |archive-date=23 November 2023 |date=3 November 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref>  With New Zealand First winning 6.08% of the party vote, he returned to Parliament for a fifth term as a list MP.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Livingstone |first=Helen |date=2023-10-14 |title=New Zealand election 2023: right-leaning coalition poised to form government after Labour swept from power – as it happened |language=en-GB |work=[[The Guardian]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/oct/14/new-zealand-election-2023-live-updates-polls-close-results-national-party-labour |access-date=2023-10-14 |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=2023 General Election: Successful candidates |url=https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/successful-candidates.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=20 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231117114239/https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/successful-candidates.html |archive-date=17 November 2023 |date=3 November 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Sixth National Government, 2023&ndash;present===
Jones was appointed the [[Minister for Oceans and Fisheries]], Minister for Regional Development, Minister for Resources, [[Associate Minister of Finance (New Zealand)|Associate Minister of Finance]] and Associate Minister of Energy in the [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand]] on 27 November 2023.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cabinet lineup for new government unveiled – who gets what? |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/503156/cabinet-lineup-for-new-government-unveiled-who-gets-what |access-date=15 December 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=24 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204122806/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/503156/cabinet-lineup-for-new-government-unveiled-who-gets-what |archive-date=4 December 2023|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 17 September 2025, Jones was elected by the New Zealand First parliamentary caucus as the party's deputy leader.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McConnell |first1=Glenn |title=De facto deputy Shane Jones officially made NZ First deputy leader |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360826543/de-facto-deputy-shane-jones-officially-made-nz-first-deputy-leader |access-date=20 September 2025 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=17 September 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250917010722/https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360826543/de-facto-deputy-shane-jones-officially-made-nz-first-deputy-leader |archive-date=17 September 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

====Oceans and Fisheries====
In late March 2024, Jones as Fisheries Minister announced the Government had halted work on legislation to create a {{Convert|620,000|km2|abbr=on}} ocean sanctuary around the [[Kermadec Islands]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Government halts plans for Kermadec Ocean Sanctuary |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/512889/government-halts-plan-for-kermadec-ocean-sanctuary |access-date=2 April 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=28 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240331131627/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/512889/government-halts-plan-for-kermadec-ocean-sanctuary |archive-date=31 March 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

In July 2024, Jones expressed interest in expanding the aquaculture sector in his capacity as Minister for Oceans and Fisheries. Jones told the news website [[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] that the aquaculture sector faced fewer barriers and offered more opportunities than the wool, dairy and meat sectors. In addition, Jones said that the aquaculture industry lacked the "cacophony of voices currently protesting New Zealand's farming footprint."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Meyer |first1=Fox |title=Jones says no more dairy |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2024/07/10/jones-says-no-more-dairy/ |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=10 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240713183858/https://newsroom.co.nz/2024/07/10/jones-says-no-more-dairy/ |archive-date=13 July 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 12 February 2025, Jones announced that the Government would be making changes to the [[Quota Management System]] including excluding ship camera footage from [[Official Information Act 1982|Official Information Act]] requests. The Green Party expressed concerns that these changes would reduce accountability in the fishing sector.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Major shake-up of fishing quota system on the way |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/business/541638/major-shake-up-of-fishing-quota-system-on-the-way |access-date=17 February 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=12 February 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250214024918/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/business/541638/major-shake-up-of-fishing-quota-system-on-the-way |archive-date=14 February 2025}}</ref>

As fisheries minister, Jones sponsored the Fisheries Amendment Bill, which proposed scrapping most legal-size limits for commercial fishing operators, allowing them to retain and sell small fish including [[Black and white snapper|snapper]], [[tarakihi]] and [[trevally]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Morrah |first1=Michael |title=Conservation Minister wants to 'get the balance right' in fishing rule changes |url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/news/politics/conservation-minister-tama-potaka-wants-to-get-the-balance-right-in-shane-jones-fishing-rule-changes |access-date=27 March 2026 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=22 March 2026}}</ref><ref name="NZH 25 Mar 2026">{{cite news |last1=Morrah |first1=Michael |title=Fisheries bill backlash: Critics say Shane Jones backdown not enough |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/shane-jones-drops-plan-to-scrap-legal-fish-size-limits-after-backlash/CZNSZRZIKVD3HK6IH6HDG7MT4E/ |access-date=25 March 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=25 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260325034730/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/shane-jones-drops-plan-to-scrap-legal-fish-size-limits-after-backlash/CZNSZRZIKVD3HK6IH6HDG7MT4E/ |archive-date=25 March 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Following pressure from Prime Minister Luxon and NZ First leader Peters, he removed the clause scrapping legal-size limits from the proposed bill in late March 2026, Jones's proposal also attracted opposition from [[Greenpeace Aotearoa New Zealand]], recreational fisheries advocacy group LegaSea and ''Ultimate Fishing'' television host Matt Watson.<ref name="NZH 25 Mar 2026" /> On 31 March, Jones's fisheries bill passed its first reading in Parliament. He used his speech to remind the National and ACT parties of the National-NZ First coalition commitment to ease regulations on the fisheries sector.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shane Jones reminds National, ACT of fisheries bill commitment |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/591156/shane-jones-reminds-national-act-of-fisheries-bill-commitment |access-date=2 April 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=31 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260331065952/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/591156/shane-jones-reminds-national-act-of-fisheries-bill-commitment |archive-date=31 March 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 18 June, the Fisheries Amendment Bill's passage to Parliament was delayed until after the [[2026 New Zealand general election]] at Jones's request.<ref>{{cite news |title=Controversial fisheries amendment Bill delayed after Shane Jones request |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/598593/controversial-fisheries-amendment-bill-delayed-after-shane-jones-request |access-date=22 June 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=18 June 2026}}</ref>

====Resources and regional development====
In June 2024, Jones announced that the Government would introduce legislation to reverse the previous Labour Government's ban on oil and gas exploration in the second half of 2024. He also confirmed that the Government would also amend legislation to make it easier for companies to get permission for oil exploration. Jones argued that reversing the ban on oil and gas exploration would help attract investment and promote economic growth and jobs. The [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]]'s co-leader [[Chlöe Swarbrick]] said that reversing the ban would worsen climate change while Labour's energy spokesperson [[Megan Woods]] criticised the Government for ignoring alternative, renewable energy sources.<ref>{{cite news |title=Bill to resume oil and gas exploration set for later this year |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/519058/bill-to-resume-oil-and-gas-exploration-set-for-later-this-year |access-date=9 June 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=9 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240609122012/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/519058/bill-to-resume-oil-and-gas-exploration-set-for-later-this-year |archive-date=9 June 2024}}</ref>

As Minister for Resources, Jones sponsored the [[Crown Minerals Amendment Act 2025]], which repealed the previous Labour government's 2018 ban on new permits for gas and oil exploration off the coast of [[Taranaki]]. The bill passed into law in late July 2025 with the support of the governing coalition parties.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Collins |first1=Louis |title=Petroleum law passes, with a last minute twist |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/568607/petroleum-law-passes-with-a-last-minute-twist |access-date=31 July 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=31 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250731061409/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/568607/petroleum-law-passes-with-a-last-minute-twist |archive-date=31 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 16 August 2025, Jones was confronted by about 100 environmental protesters during his visit to [[Whangārei]]. The protesters voiced opposition to two [[Fast-track Approvals Act 2024|fast-track]] projects including the construction of a marina in [[Waipiro Bay]] and proposed offshore sand mining in [[Bream Bay]]. In response, Jones argued the fast-track projects would aid the economic development of the [[Northland Region]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=de Graaf |first1=Peter |title=Protesters confront Resources Minister Shane Jones in Whangārei over fast-track projects |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/570193/protesters-confront-resources-minister-shane-jones-in-whangarei-over-fast-track-projects |access-date=18 August 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=16 August 2025}}</ref>

On 1 September 2025, Jones confirmed that the Government would allocate NZ$30{{Nbsp}}million from the Regional Development Fund to provide loans to supporting regional airlines.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Government announces $30 million loans to small airlines to support regional routes, approves funding for interlining |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/government-announces-30-million-loans-to-small-airlines-to-support-regional-routes-approves-funding-for-interlining/4GX4UXIKUVBNLKFU57LJ6CFNNE/ |access-date=2 September 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=1 September 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250902043800/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/government-announces-30-million-loans-to-small-airlines-to-support-regional-routes-approves-funding-for-interlining/4GX4UXIKUVBNLKFU57LJ6CFNNE/ |archive-date=2 September 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In late January 2026, [[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] reported that Jones had been involved in New Zealand's negotiations to supply critical rare earth minerals to the United States as part of the multilateral Minerals Security Partnership.  Jones had linked the deal to attracting foreign investment in the mining sector amidst a global demand for rare earth minerals. The [[Waitangi Tribunal]] had expressed concern that a "backdoor" bilateral deal with the United States without Māori input would violate the [[New Zealand Crown]]'s [[Treaty of Waitangi]] obligations to Māori.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Meyer |first1=Fox |title=NZ in talks with US on Trump minerals deal |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2026/01/30/nz-in-talks-with-us-on-trump-minerals-deal/ |access-date=24 April 2026 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=30 January 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260204015439/https://newsroom.co.nz/2026/01/30/nz-in-talks-with-us-on-trump-minerals-deal/ |archive-date=4 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> By April 2026, mineral negotiations had resumed. Jones voiced support for a multilateral agreement, saying it would be preferable for critics who balked at the idea of a bilateral export agreement with the [[Second presidency of Donald Trump|second Trump Administration]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Meyer |first1=Fox |title=Officials directed to resume US critical minerals trade deal |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2026/04/21/officials-directed-to-resume-us-critical-minerals-trade-deal/ |access-date=24 April 2026 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=21 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260421051216/https://newsroom.co.nz/2026/04/21/officials-directed-to-resume-us-critical-minerals-trade-deal/ |archive-date=21 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In March 2026, Jones was challenged to a debate with Sir [[Ian Taylor (New Zealand businessman)|Ian Taylor]] in regards to the proposed Santana Minerals [[Bendigo, New Zealand|Bendigo]]-[[Ophir, New Zealand|Ophir]] gold mine.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Hepburn |first=Steve |date=13 March 2026 |title=Sir Ian, Jones to face off over gold mine |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/regions/central-otago/sir-ian-jones-face-over-gold-mine |access-date=27 March 2026|work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Hepburn |first=Steve |date=7 March 2026 |title=Jones gives ground rules for Sir Ian debate |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/regions/central-otago/jones-gives-ground-rules-sir-ian-debate |access-date=27 March 2026 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |language=en}}</ref> Despite initially accepting, Jones pulled out of the debate less than two weeks before the scheduled date.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Shaw |first=Ruby |date=27 March 2026 |title=Jones pulls out of debate, blames media |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/regions/central-otago/jones-pulls-out-debate-blames-media |access-date=27 March 2026 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |language=en}}</ref>

In mid-June 2026, Jones sought retrospective approval from the [[New Zealand Cabinet]] for an additional NZ$33,068 in taxpayer funds to cover a February 2025 trip to Canada to attend the [[Prospectors and Developers Association of Canada]]'s mining convention. While originally allocated a ministerial budget of $30,000, Jones's Canadian travel expenses had cost a total of NZ$63,000 due to the inclusion of a private limousine and two [[Qantas]] business-class flights (worth $40,000).<ref name="Lynch 19 Jun 2026">{{cite news |last1=Lynch |first1=Jenna |title=Winston Peters defends Shane Jones' travel cost blowout, says budget was 'low' |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360994527/shane-jones-luxury-flights-booked-weeks-cabinet-approved-cheaper-ones |access-date=22 June 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=19 June 2026}}</ref> Jones's travel expenses drew criticism from [[Minister of Finance (New Zealand)|Finance Minister]] [[Nicola Willis]], who said he had made "significant errors." In response, Jones attributed the spending blowup to an administrative error by government officials.<ref name="Moir 18 Jun 2026">{{cite news |last1=Moir |first1=Jo |title=Winston Peters defends Shane Jones' $63k travel bill including private limo on standby for 24 hours |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/598565/winston-peters-defends-shane-jones-63k-travel-bill-including-private-limo-on-standby-for-24-hours |access-date=22 June 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=18 June 2026}}</ref> NZ First leader Peters also defended Jones's spending, claiming that the government travel budget for ministers was too low.<ref name="Moir 19 Jun 2026" /><ref name="Lynch 19 Jun 2026" />

====Associate energy====
In early August 2024, Jones accused electrical utility companies of exploiting soaring energy bills and said that the Government was seeking advice on potential regulatory intervention in the energy sector.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shane Jones accuses big power companies of profiteering |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/524482/shane-jones-accuses-big-power-companies-of-profiteering |access-date=11 August 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=8 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240809013817/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/524482/shane-jones-accuses-big-power-companies-of-profiteering |archive-date=9 August 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

In April 2026, Jones confirmed that the New Zealand Government would allocate over $20 million from the Regional Infrastructure Fund to recommissioning [[Diesel fuel|diesel]] storage tanks at the former [[Marsden Point Oil Refinery]]'s import and storage terminal. This investment would support 90 million litres of fuel storage.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Marsden Point to get diesel storage capacity boost |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/591355/marsden-point-to-get-diesel-storage-capacity-boost |access-date=4 April 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=2 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260402014149/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/591355/marsden-point-to-get-diesel-storage-capacity-boost |archive-date=2 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

====Miscellaneous====
In late June 2025, Jones confirmed that he was drafting a member's bill into Parliament seeking to force the Māori [[iwi]] (tribe) [[Ngāpuhi]] into a single commercial [[Treaty of Waitangi]] settlement.  He said that the bill would focus on good governance, economic development and would exclude [[indigenous sovereignty]], which he described as a "diversion" that the New Zealand taxpayer could not afford.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shane Jones says bill is being drafted forcing Ngāpuhi into a single commercial settlement |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/564970/shane-jones-says-bill-is-being-drafted-forcing-ngapuhi-into-a-single-commercial-settlement |access-date=26 June 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=24 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250623223055/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/564970/shane-jones-says-bill-is-being-drafted-forcing-ngapuhi-into-a-single-commercial-settlement |archive-date=23 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Labour MP [[Peeni Henare]], who is of Ngāpuhi descent, described Jones's proposal as misguided and said that it would breach the "good faith" provisions of Treaty settlements. On 24 June, Jones stated that NZ First would campaign on how taxpayer money was being used for Treaty settlements at the 2026 general election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Treaty Negotiations Minister says settlements with iwi can't be 'open ended' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/565012/treaty-negotiations-minister-says-settlements-with-iwi-can-t-be-open-ended |access-date=26 June 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=24 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250624120419/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/565012/treaty-negotiations-minister-says-settlements-with-iwi-can-t-be-open-ended |archive-date=24 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

====2026 general election====
During New Zealand First's election campaign launch on 19 July, Jones confirmed that he would be contesting the [[Northland (electorate)|Northland electorate]]  at the [[2026 New Zealand general election]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=NZ First promises to invest in oil, gas surveys |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/742372/nz-first-promises-to-invest-in-oil-gas-surveys |access-date=19 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=19 July 2026}}</ref>

== Political views ==
===Conscience votes===
Jones voted in favour of the [[Marriage (Definition of Marriage) Amendment Act 2013|Marriage (Definition of Marriage) Amendment Bill]] to legalise [[same-sex marriage in New Zealand]] in 2012 and 2013.<ref name="MDOMA1">{{cite web |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10830383 |title=Marriage equality bill: How MPs voted |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=29 August 2012 |accessdate=6 December 2023 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160315113055/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10830383 |archivedate=15 March 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="MDOMA2">{{cite news |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10871107 |title=Gay bill bolts over hurdle |first=Isaac |last=Davison |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=14 March 2013 |accessdate=6 December 2023 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303185250/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10871107 |archivedate=3 March 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="MDOMA3">{{cite news |title=Gay marriage: How MPs voted |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/gay-marriage/news/article.cfm?c_id=1503276&objectid=10878241 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=18 April 2013 |accessdate=6 December 2023 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160331174829/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/gay-marriage/news/article.cfm?c_id=1503276&objectid=10878241 |archivedate=31 March 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref> He supported the [[End of Life Choice Act 2019|End of Life Choice Bill]] at all stages in 2017 and 2019.<ref name="EOLC1">{{cite news |title=Euthanasia bill passes first reading in Parliament |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11959128 |accessdate=6 December 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=17 December 2017}}</ref><ref name="EOLC2">{{cite news |last1=Cooke |first1=Henry |title=Euthanasia bill passes second reading |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/113795416/euthanasia-bill-passes-second-reading |accessdate=6 December 2023 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=26 June 2019}}</ref><ref name="EOLC3">{{cite web |title=End of Life Choice Bill — Third Reading |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/combined/HansDeb_20191113_20191113_16 |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |accessdate=6 December 2023|date=13 November 2019}}</ref> He supported the [[Abortion Legislation Act 2020|Abortion Legislation Bill]] at its first and second readings in 2019 and 2020.<ref name="ALA1">{{cite news |last1=Walls |first1=Jason |title=How Members of Parliament voted in the first reading of the Abortion Legislation Bill |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12256951 |accessdate=6 December 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=8 August 2019}}</ref><ref name="ALA2">{{cite news |last1=Cheng |first1=Derek |title=Abortion law reform passes next parliamentary hurdle comfortably |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12313615 |accessdate=6 December 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=3 March 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200605133914/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12313615|archive-date=5 June 2020|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Demographics and immigration ===
Jones attracted criticism when he made a series of anti-Indian remarks in October and November 2019 and again in February 2020. In response to members of the [[Indian New Zealanders|Indian New Zealand community]]'s criticism of [[Immigration New Zealand]]'s recent decision to tighten partnership visas for those on [[Arranged marriage in the Indian subcontinent|arranged marriage]]s,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Walters |first1=Laura |title=Immigration NZ partnership visa policy labelled 'racist' |url=https://www.newsroom.co.nz/2019/10/22/871682/immigration-nz-partnership-visa-policy-labelled-racist-unsympathetic# |access-date=7 November 2019 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=22 October 2019}}</ref> Jones had said: 
{{blockquote|I would just say to the activists from the Indian community, tame down your rhetoric, you have no legitimate expectations in my view to bring your whole village to New Zealand and if you don't like it and you're threatening to go home – catch the next flight home.<ref name="NZH 26 Oct 2019">{{cite news |title=Indian association demands public apology from Shane Jones |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12279923 |access-date=26 October 2019 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=26 October 2019}}</ref>}}

Jones's comments were condemned by the Waitakere Indian Association, who called on Ardern and Minister for Ethnic Communities [[Jenny Salesa]] to demand a public apology from Jones and to address the Indian community's concerns.<ref>{{cite news |title=WIA condemns statement by Shane Jones |url=http://community.scoop.co.nz/2019/10/wia-condemns-statement-by-shane-jones/ |access-date=26 October 2019 |work=Waitakere Indian Association |agency=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]] |date=25 October 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Bhatia |first1=Ripue |title=NZ First MP Shane Jones under fire for 'derogatory' comments towards Indian community |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/116892733/nz-first-mp-shane-jones-under-fire-for-derogatory-comments-towards-indian-community |access-date=26 October 2019 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=25 October 2019}}</ref> A rally in protest of Jones's remarks was held on 3 November 2019 by members of the Migrant Workers Association and Love Aotearoa Hate Racism.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Tokalau |first1=Torika |title=Migrant and racism action group calls for Shane Jones' resignation for 'blatant racism' |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/117126408/migrant-and-racism-action-group-call-for-shane-jones-resignation-for-blatant-racism-comments |access-date=5 November 2019 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=3 November 2019}}</ref> 
Jones's remarks were also condemned by the broadcaster [[Patrick Gower]], who described Jones as a "gutless wonder."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Quinlivan |first1=Mark |title='Gutless': Patrick Gower unleashes on Shane Jones following migrant comments |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2019/11/gutless-patrick-gower-unleashes-on-shane-jones-following-migrant-comments.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191104225209/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2019/11/gutless-patrick-gower-unleashes-on-shane-jones-following-migrant-comments.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=4 November 2019 |access-date=5 November 2019 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=4 November 2019}}</ref> Ardern, Trade Minister [[Damien O'Connor]], and [[Minister of Immigration (New Zealand)|Immigration Minister]] [[Iain Lees-Galloway]] have disavowed Jones's remarks as not representative of the New Zealand Government.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shane Jones' comments about NZ Indian community 'not the position' of Labour, Jacinda Ardern says |url=https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/shane-jones-comments-nz-indian-community-not-position-labour-jacinda-ardern-says |access-date=5 November 2019 |work=[[1News]] |date=4 November 2019}}</ref><ref name="Newshub 5 Nov 2019">{{cite news |last1=Small |first1=Zane |title='Bollywood overreaction': Shane Jones digs in after angering Indian community |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2019/11/that-s-a-bollywood-overreaction-shane-jones-digs-in-after-angering-indian-community.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191104225208/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2019/11/that-s-a-bollywood-overreaction-shane-jones-digs-in-after-angering-indian-community.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=4 November 2019 |access-date=5 November 2019 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=5 November 2019}}</ref> On 5 November 2019, Jones described the community response as a "[[Bollywood]] reaction" and claimed that he was speaking for New Zealanders who were anxious about immigration.<ref name="Newshub 5 Nov 2019" /> On 6 November 2019, the Government reversed the partnership visa decision, restoring the exception for non-resident Indian marriages.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bradford |first1=Katie |title=Controversial partnership visa decision set to be reversed by Immigration New Zealand |url=https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/controversial-partnership-visa-decision-set-reversed-immigration-new-zealand |access-date=7 November 2019 |work=[[1News]] |date=6 November 2019}}</ref>

The following year, Jones claimed in a television interview that immigration was placing "enormous stress" on the country's social and economic infrastructure and that the large number of international students from India had ruined New Zealand tertiary institutions.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Satherley |first1=Dan |title=Shane Jones says Indian students have 'ruined' NZ academic institutions |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/02/shane-jones-says-indian-students-have-ruined-nz-academic-institutions.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200303002025/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/02/shane-jones-says-indian-students-have-ruined-nz-academic-institutions.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=3 March 2020 |access-date=3 March 2020 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=29 February 2020}}</ref> Jones's remarks were criticised by Ardern, the Waitakere Indian Association, National Party leader [[Simon Bridges]], [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] co-leader [[James Shaw (New Zealand politician)|James Shaw]], and Labour MP [[Iain Lees-Galloway]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCullough |first1=Yvette |title=PM Jacinda Ardern publicly reprimands Shane Jones over Indian immigrant remarks |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/top/410799/pm-jacinda-ardern-publicly-reprimands-shane-jones-over-indian-immigrant-remarks |access-date=3 March 2020 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=3 March 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Lynch |first1=Jenna |title=Major ruction in Government over Shane Jones' 'racist' Indian remarks |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/03/major-ruction-in-government-over-shane-jones-racist-indian-remarks.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200303093436/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/03/major-ruction-in-government-over-shane-jones-racist-indian-remarks.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=3 March 2020 |access-date=3 March 2020 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=3 March 2020}}</ref> The [[Human Rights Commission (New Zealand)#Race relations|Race Relations Commissioner]] [[Meng Foon]] also condemned them as "racist, ignorant and harmful." Jones defended his comments, claiming that members of the Indian community were exploiting their own people.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Shane Jones' Indian comments 'racist, ignorant, harmful' – Race Relations Commissioner |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/03/shane-jones-indian-comments-racist-ignorant-harmful-race-relations-commissioner.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200303002026/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/03/shane-jones-indian-comments-racist-ignorant-harmful-race-relations-commissioner.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=3 March 2020 |access-date=3 March 2020 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=3 March 2020}}</ref>

On 29 January 2025, Jones faced criticism for shouting "Send the Mexicans home!" during a debate in Parliament. He later said "If these foreign-born New Zealanders are not going to show respect to the culture of New Zealand, then I'm going to call them out." [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] immigration spokesperson [[Ricardo Menéndez March]] condemned Jones's comments, arguing that they emboldened [[xenophobia]] and were intended to spark division. He compared Jones's remarks to rhetoric used by U.S. President [[Donald Trump]]. The Green Party formally raised concerns with Prime Minister Christopher Luxon, calling for accountability.<ref>{{Cite web |title=NZ First MP shouts 'send the Mexicans home' – as identity war sparks in Parliament |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360563844/nz-first-mp-shouts-send-mexicans-home-identity-war-sparks-parliament |access-date=2025-01-29 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-01-29 |title=Greens ask PM to condemn 'outwardly racist' rhetoric |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/540293/greens-ask-pm-to-condemn-outwardly-racist-rhetoric |access-date=2025-01-29 |website=[[RNZ]] |language=en-nz}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=NZ First accuses Greens of 'pearl clutching' and 'faux outrage' as culture war hostilities escalate |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360564434/shane-jones-doubles-down-controversial-comments-calling-it-mexican-stand |access-date=2025-01-29 |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] }}</ref>  In response, Luxon refused to confirm whether he would discipline Jones but advised other MPs to "watch their language." After the Mexican Embassy raised concerns about Jones's remarks, Peters met with the Mexican Ambassador at [[Waitangi, Northland|Waitangi]] during the Waitangi Day events in early February 2025.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Mexican Embassy wades in after NZ First minister's comments: PM Christopher Luxon repeats 'rhetoric' warning |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/mexican-embassy-wades-in-after-nz-first-ministers-comments-pm-christopher-luxon-repeats-rhetoric-warning/C64ALTELQFF37AV25REJ2OU5RQ/ |access-date=30 January 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=30 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250130205835/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/mexican-embassy-wades-in-after-nz-first-ministers-comments-pm-christopher-luxon-repeats-rhetoric-warning/C64ALTELQFF37AV25REJ2OU5RQ/ |archive-date=30 January 2025}}</ref>

In advance of the 2025 New Zealand First annual meeting, Jones complained that immigration was changing the country's demographics. He remarked that New Zealand was "changing irreversibly" on a cultural level, citing that the most common baby names were now "[[Singh]]" and "[[Patel]]". [[Indian New Zealanders]] interviewed by [[Radio New Zealand|RNZ News]] rejected the comments, noting that Indian immigration has a long history in New Zealand, with some families going back over a century and that Jones's "racist" comments exemplified divisive rhetoric that has been used for decades to exclude immigrants and their descendants. Jones did not respond when asked for comment.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-09-11 |title=Indian community slams Shane Jones' 'Singh, Patel' barb |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/indonz/572764/indian-community-slams-shane-jones-singh-patel-barb |access-date=2026-01-03 |website=RNZ |language=en-nz}}</ref> In a later opinion article for ''[[The Post (Wellington)|The Post]]'', columnist Ben Thomas criticised Jones's remarks, writing that he "didn’t so much dip his toe in the topic of increasing Indian immigration as cannonball off a railbridge into it" and dismissing Jones's comments as a "[[diatribe]]".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Thomas |first=Ben |date=2 Jan 2026 |title=How imported hoaxes and opinions could impact this year's election |url=https://www.thepost.co.nz/nz-news/360925890/how-imported-hoaxes-and-opinions-could-impact-years-election |access-date=3 January 2026 |work=[[The Post (Wellington)|The Post]]}}</ref>

In mid April 2026, Jones attracted controversy after making racially-tinged remarks targeting Indian migrants during an interview with the online radio station [[Reality Check Radio]]. Jones had opposed the proposed [[New Zealand–India Free Trade Agreement|India-New Zealand agreement]] on the grounds that it would allow "unfettered" immigration into New Zealand, stating: "I don't care how much criticism I get. I am never going to agree with a [[butter chicken]] tsunami coming to New Zealand."<ref name="1News 20 Apr 2026">{{cite news |last1=Day |first1=Tom |title=Jones' 'butter chicken tsunami' comment 'scaremongering', says PM |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/04/20/jones-butter-chicken-tsunami-comment-scaremongering-says-pm/ |access-date=24 April 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=20 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260421031413/https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/04/20/jones-butter-chicken-tsunami-comment-scaremongering-says-pm/ |archive-date=21 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="RNZ 21 Apr 2026">{{cite news |title=New Zealand First's Shane Jones defends comparing India FTA to 'butter chicken tsunami' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/592942/new-zealand-first-s-shane-jones-defends-comparing-india-fta-to-butter-chicken-tsunami |access-date=24 April 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=21 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260422233845/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/592942/new-zealand-first-s-shane-jones-defends-comparing-india-fta-to-butter-chicken-tsunami |archive-date=22 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Jones's remarks drew criticism from across the New Zealand political spectrum including Prime Minister [[Christopher Luxon]], Deputy Prime Minister and [[ACT New Zealand|ACT party]] leader [[David Seymour]], [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] leader [[Chris Hipkins]], [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] MP [[Carlos Cheung]], ACT MP [[Parmjeet Parmar]] and Immigration Minister [[Erica Stanford]], who described them as "unhelpful" and offensive to Indian New Zealanders.<ref name="1News 20 Apr 2026" /><ref name="RNZ 21 Apr 2026" /><ref name="Blessen 23 Apr 2026">{{cite news |last1=Blessen|first1=Tom |title='Distasteful': Anger over Jones' 'butter chicken tsunami' barb |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/indonz/592985/distasteful-anger-over-jones-butter-chicken-tsunami-barb |access-date=24 April 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=23 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260423025319/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/indonz/592985/distasteful-anger-over-jones-butter-chicken-tsunami-barb |archive-date=23 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Several Indian community leaders including Auckland Indian Association president Shanti Patel, New Zealand Indian Central Association Veer Khar,  NZ Indian Business Association general secretary Jaspreet Kandhari and the New Zealand Sikh Society Hamilton's former president Yugraj Singh Mahil also expressed disapproval and condemnation of Jones's remarks.<ref name="Blessen 23 Apr 2026" /> In response, Jones defended his comments and reiterated his criticism of the India-New Zealand free trade agreement, stating "immigration has been snuck into the free trade deal in a way that does not reflect the expectations of Kiwis".<ref name="RNZ 21 Apr 2026" /> Jones's remarks also attracted coverage in the Indian and international media.<ref>{{cite news |title='I get cut through by hyperbole': NZ minister Shane Jones refuses to tone down 'butter chicken tsunami' remark |url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/i-get-cut-through-by-hyperbole-nz-minister-shane-jones-refuses-to-tone-down-butter-chicken-tsunami-remark/articleshow/130439575.cms |access-date=24 April 2026 |work=[[The Times of India]] |date=22 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260423014526/https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/i-get-cut-through-by-hyperbole-nz-minister-shane-jones-refuses-to-tone-down-butter-chicken-tsunami-remark/articleshow/130439575.cms |archive-date=23 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=NZ minister slammed for 'butter chicken' comments |url=https://www.taipeitimes.com/News/world/archives/2026/04/23/2003856107 |access-date=24 April 2026 |work=[[Taipei Times]] |agency=[[Agence France-Presse]] |date=23 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260423032016/https://www.taipeitimes.com/News/world/archives/2026/04/23/2003856107 |archive-date=23 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Withers |first1=Tracey |title=New Zealand PM defends Indian FTA ahead of signing next week |url=https://theedgemalaysia.com/node/800497 |access-date=24 April 2026 |work=[[The Edge (Malaysia)|The Edge]] |agency=[[Bloomberg News]] |date=20 April 2026}}</ref>

===Judiciary===
In mid-February 2024, Jones criticised the [[Supreme Court of New Zealand]] for permitting climate activist [[Mike Smith (activist)|Mike Smith]] to pursue legal action against several polluters, describing the ruling as the "[[Americanisation]]" of New Zealand's judiciary. Jones also claimed that the tobacco industry was being demonised by politicians, stating "that there's a great deal of catastrophisation and tainting going on with these people in the tobacco industry. They're running an industry that still, the last time I checked, was a legitimate part of the economy."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Daalder |first1=Marc |title=Shane Jones criticised over attack on Supreme Court ruling |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2024/02/19/shane-jones-criticised-over-attack-on-supreme-court-ruling/ |access-date=11 April 2024 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=19 February 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240317063119/https://newsroom.co.nz/2024/02/19/shane-jones-criticised-over-attack-on-supreme-court-ruling/|archive-date=17 March 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Local government reform===
In late June 2025, Jones suggested abolishing [[Regional councils of New Zealand|regional councils]] while addressing a [[Local Government New Zealand]] conference. He said that his party NZ First did not see a "compelling case" for regional councils to continue after the National-led coalition government had replaced the [[Resource Management Act 1991]]. Jones also said that the [[Otago Regional Council|Otago]] and [[Waikato Regional Council]]s were obstructing economic development by blocking mining and marine farming permits. In response, the [[Bay of Plenty Regional Council]]'s chair Doug Leeder said that regional councils played an important role in managing natural resources, flood protection, public transport delivery, emergency management functions, regional planning and biosecurity.<ref>{{cite news |title=Minister Shane Jones accuses council of being 'iwi back office' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/565090/minister-shane-jones-accuses-council-of-being-iwi-back-office |access-date=27 June 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=25 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250624232339/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/565090/minister-shane-jones-accuses-council-of-being-iwi-back-office |archive-date=24 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Māori issues===
In late January 2024, Jones along with his New Zealand First and National parties colleagues including Winston Peters and Prime Minister [[Christopher Luxon]] attended the [[Rātana Church]]'s special annual hui (meeting).<ref>{{cite news |last1=de Silva |first1=Tommy |title=This week's hui at Rātana pā explained |url= https://thespinoff.co.nz/atea/23-01-2024/this-weeks-hui-at-ratana-pa-explained |access-date=26 January 2024 |work=[[The Spinoff]] |date=23 January 2024 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240124050327/https://thespinoff.co.nz/atea/23-01-2024/this-weeks-hui-at-ratana-pa-explained |archive-date=24 January 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> During his speech, Jones expressed support for reviewing the powers of the [[Waitangi Tribunal]], stating: "An institution that's been around for 50 years should not expect to continue on uncritically for another set of decades without being reviewed.".<ref>{{cite news |title=Rātana: Prime Minister Christopher Luxon warned over te Tiriti o Waitangi |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/507456/ratana-prime-minister-christopher-luxon-warned-over-te-tiriti-o-waitangi |access-date=26 January 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=24 January 2024 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240124090301/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/507456/ratana-prime-minister-christopher-luxon-warned-over-te-tiriti-o-waitangi |archive-date=24 January 2024}}</ref>

During his 2024 [[Waitangi Day]] speech on 6 February, Jones defended the Government's proposed [[Treaty Principles Bill]] and rejected opposition claims that the Government was degrading [[tino rangatiratanga]] (self determination). He also said that the Government was funding [[wānanga]] (Māori tertiary institutions) and [[marae]] (meeting houses).<ref>{{cite news |title=Waitangi Day 2024: Government denies it's 'delegitimising' Māori, blames previous government |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/508457/waitangi-day-2024-government-denies-it-s-delegitimising-maori-blames-previous-government |access-date=7 February 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=5 February 2024 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240206155728/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/508457/waitangi-day-2024-government-denies-it-s-delegitimising-maori-blames-previous-government |archive-date=6 February 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> During [[Māori King Movement|Māori King]] [[Tūheitia Paki]]'s 18th ''Koroneihana'' (coronation anniversary celebration) in mid-August 2024,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Moir |first1=Jo |title=Politicians of all stripes to attend Māori King's Koroneihana – with one notable absence |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/525505/politicians-of-all-stripes-to-attend-maori-king-s-koroneihana-with-one-notable-absence |access-date=22 August 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=19 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240820171009/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/525505/politicians-of-all-stripes-to-attend-maori-king-s-koroneihana-with-one-notable-absence |archive-date=20 August 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> Jones reiterated that NZ First would not support the Treaty Principles Bill beyond its first reading.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Moir |first1=Jo |title=No lifeline for Seymour's Treaty Principles Bill after Koroneihana |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/on-the-inside/525601/no-lifeline-for-seymour-s-treaty-principles-bill-after-koroneihana |access-date=22 August 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=20 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240821125612/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/on-the-inside/525601/no-lifeline-for-seymour-s-treaty-principles-bill-after-koroneihana |archive-date=21 August 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

In mid April 2024, the Waitangi Tribunal summoned ACT MP and Minister for Children [[Karen Chhour]] to an urgent hearing on the Government's plans to [[Oranga Tamariki (Repeal of Section 7AA) Amendment Act 2025|repeal Section 7AA]] of the [[Oranga Tamariki Act 1989]]. In response, Jones told [[Radio Waatea|Waatea News]] during an interview that "the Waitangi Tribunal has no business running its operations as some sort of star chamber delivering summons for ministers to rock up and be cross-examined or grilled." Jones's remarks were criticised as inappropriate by the Māori Law Society, who complained to Prime Minister [[Christopher Luxon]] and [[Attorney-General of New Zealand|Attorney-General]] [[Judith Collins]]. They asked whether Jones's remarks breached the Cabinet Manual and for the Government to uphold the integrity of the judicial arm.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hurihangangui |first1=Te Aniwa |title='Inappropriate': Māori lawyers write to PM over Shane Jones comments |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/17/inappropriate-maori-lawyers-write-to-pm-over-shane-jones-comments/ |access-date=21 April 2024 |work=[[1News]] |publisher=[[TVNZ]] |date=17 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240421120652/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/17/inappropriate-maori-lawyers-write-to-pm-over-shane-jones-comments/ |archive-date=21 April 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Tobacco===
Jones has attracted scrutiny due to his links to the tobacco industry and New Zealand First successfully pressing for the repeal of smoke free legislation as part of its [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand#Coalition agreement|coalition agreement]] with the National Party. According to [[Radio New Zealand]], [[Philip Morris International]]'s external relations director and former NZ First staffer Api Dawson attended Jones's swearing-in ceremony at Parliament in late 2023. Jones also confirmed that Dawson was involved in "soundings" about the party's tobacco policy. In March 2024, when Jones was asked if he was complying with the [[WHO Framework Convention on Tobacco Control]], which "obliges its parties to protect policy from tobacco industry influence and be completely transparent in its dealings with the industry", he stated that he did not know about it and was not interested in it.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Newton |first1=Kate |title=NZ First Minister Shane Jones says he doesn't know or care about tobacco industry transparency rules |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/in-depth/511054/nz-first-minister-shane-jones-says-he-doesn-t-know-or-care-about-tobacco-industry-transparency-rules |access-date=11 April 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=7 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240329163749/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/in-depth/511054/nz-first-minister-shane-jones-says-he-doesn-t-know-or-care-about-tobacco-industry-transparency-rules |archive-date=29 March 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Jones has seven children with his first wife Ngāreta,<ref name=":0" /> from whom he separated in 2011;<ref name=partners/> she died from cancer in 2015.<ref>{{Cite news|date=7 September 2015 |title=Lifelong worker for change Ngareta Jones dies |url=https://waateanews.com/2015/09/07/lifelong-worker-for-change-ngareta-jones-dies/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |work=[[Radio Waatea|Waatea News]] |language=en-NZ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250111081851/https://waateanews.com/2015/09/07/lifelong-worker-for-change-ngareta-jones-dies/|archive-date=11 January 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> Jones began a relationship with former beauty queen Dorothy (Dot) Pumipi in 2011 while she was his campaign manager;<ref name=partners>{{Cite news |last1=Vance |first1=Andrea |last2=Watkins |first2=Tracy |date=31 August 2013 |title=Labour of love for the partners |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/ipad-editors-picks/9111399/Labour-of-love-for-the-partners |access-date=17 November 2022 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250111070655/https://www.stuff.co.nz/ipad-editors-picks/9111399/Labour-of-love-for-the-partners|archive-date=11 January 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> they married in 2018.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shane Jones gets married in Rarotonga |date=6 January 2018 |url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/shane-jones-gets-married-in-rarotonga/KA6KKQ7FTSEBTLEILBB2MWFIGQ/ |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=29 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250111123220/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/shane-jones-gets-married-in-rarotonga/KA6KKQ7FTSEBTLEILBB2MWFIGQ/|archive-date=11 January 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 5 April 2025, Jones and Dot were confronted by a man at [[Auckland Airport]], who shouted "profane abuse" about Jones and New Zealand First leader Winston Peters. When Dot attempted to intervene between the two men, her hand hit his nose, and the man allegedly grabbed her shoulder, causing her to lose her balance and fall. The couple subsequently filed a police report against the man.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fisher |first1=David |title=Shane Jones and wife Dot say she was grabbed after altercation at Auckland Airport |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/shane-jones-and-wife-dot-say-she-was-grabbed-after-altercation-at-auckland-airport/55BWCTU66RCNTLIIDIPN47YHWA/ |access-date=7 April 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=6 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250407022755/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/shane-jones-and-wife-dot-say-she-was-grabbed-after-altercation-at-auckland-airport/55BWCTU66RCNTLIIDIPN47YHWA/ |archive-date=7 April 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

Jones has been photographed wearing a "[[Make America Great Again|Make New Zealand Great Again]]" hat.<ref>{{Cite web |title=NZ First targets 'woke' legislation it previously helped make law |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2025/03/08/nz-first-targets-woke-legislation-it-previously-helped-make-law/ |access-date=2026-01-05 |website=Te Ao Māori News |language=en}}</ref>

